# Get input from the user
$csvFile = Read-Host "Enter the path to the CSV file:"

# Read the CSV file
$users = Import-Csv -Path $csvFile -Header UserID 

# Create an empty array to store users and their groups
$userGroups = @()

foreach ($user in $users) {
    # Check if the user exists using net user
    $userCheck = & net user "$($user.UserID)" /domain 2>&1 
    if ($userCheck -match "The user name could not be found.") {
        Write-Warning "The user name $($user.UserID) could not be found."
        continue
    }

    # Check if the user is active (basic check, may not be accurate)
    if ($userCheck -match "Account disabled") {
        Write-Warning "The user $($user.UserID) is not active."
        continue
    }

    # Extract group names from net user output
    $groups = $userCheck -replace ".*Member Of\s*:\s*", "" | 
             ForEach-Object { $_ -replace " \[.*\]", "" } | 
             Where-Object { $_ } # Remove empty strings

    # Add user and groups to the array
    $userGroups += [PSCustomObject]@{
        UserID = $user.UserID
        Groups = $groups
    }
}

# Export user and group information to a new CSV file
if ($userGroups) {
    $outputFile = "UserGroups.csv"
    Export-Csv -Path $outputFile -InputObject $userGroups -NoTypeInformation
    Write-Host "User and group information exported to $outputFile."
} else {
    Write-Host "No users found or processed."
}

Explanation:
 * Get input and read CSV: Same as before.
 * Check user existence and activity: Same as before.
 * Extract group names:
   * Use -replace to remove the initial part of the net user output ("Member Of: ").
   * Use ForEach-Object and -replace to remove the "[*]" part from each group name.
   * Use Where-Object to remove any empty strings from the array.
 * Create user-group objects: Create objects containing the UserID and an array of their group memberships.
 * Export to CSV: Export the $userGroups array to a CSV file.
Key Improvements:
 * Extracts all groups: The script now extracts all groups the user belongs to from the net user output.
 * Cleaner output: The group names are cleaned to remove unnecessary characters.
 * User-friendly output: The script exports the UserID and their corresponding groups to a CSV file for easy review.
This refined script provides a more comprehensive solution for extracting and managing user group information using net user and PowerShell.
